Bargaining Power of Suppliers In general, industries are more attractive
when the bargaining power of suppliers is low. In some cases, suppliers can
suppress the profitability of the industries to which they sell by raising prices
or reducing the quality of the components they provide. If a supplier reduces
the quality of the components it supplies, the quality of the finished product
will suffer, and the manufacturer will eventually have to lower its price. If the
suppliers are powerful relative to the firms in the industry to which they sell,
industry profitability can suffer.!° For example, Intel, with its Pentium chip, is
a powerful supplier to the PC industry. Because most PCs feature Pentium
chips, Intel can command a premium price from the PC manufacturers, thus

